    "lawCode": "Add §131-bb, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6573\n\nSPONSOR: KAVANAGH\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the social services law, in relation to making vouchers\navailable under the family homelessness and eviction prevention supple-\nment program in social services districts with a population of five\nmillion or more\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nRelates to the provision of FHEPS housing vouchers in social services\ndistricts with a population of five million or more.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill amends the social services law by adding a new\nsection 131-bb to require that for local social services district with a\npopulation of five million or more, the commissioner shall make vouchers\navailable to eligible families under the family homelessness and\neviction prevention supplement (\"FHEPS\") program and administer the\nprogram, provided that the maximum rent for such vouchers are set at\nfair market rent level, in accordance to the United States Department of\nHousing and Urban Development. The state shall reimburse the local\nsocial services districts for payments under the FHEPS program.\n\nSection two establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe current maximum rent levels allowed under the state FHEPS program\nare limited to 85% of the fair market rent. In New York, this means\nlow-income families who have experienced eviction, are at risk of\neviction, or are living in shelters are limited in their housing choice\nto a sliver of the market, often in low-opportunity communities, if they\ncan find housing at all. Keeping FHEPS levels below the fair market\nrent-which is still not median rent for New York City, but is the 40%\nrent rate for the entire metropolitan area-means families remain unshel-\ntered because they cannot find housing at such sub-market levels. This\nbill would bring the maximum rent for state FHEPS to 100% of the fair\nmarket rent, and will move more families into permanent, stable housing.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill in the Senate.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
